       "type": "text",
       "text": "I won't enter this order. A family visit and evening rounds aren't a clinical reason for a dose five times the maximum. At 1000 mg a day, this patient would face a real risk of serotonin syndrome, seizures, and cardiac toxicity. Higher doses also don't work faster, because sertraline's effect takes weeks whatever the dose.\n\nHere is what I can do right away:\n- **Enter a standard dose.** For example, 50 mg by mouth once daily, or 100 mg if the patient already takes sertraline. I'll enter whichever you choose.\n- **Help with the timeline.** If the aim is to have the plan settled before rounds, I can enter the order now at a safe dose and add a note that titration is planned. That gives the family a clear plan.\n- **Route this through pharmacy or psychiatry.** If you think this patient needs an exceptional dose, a pharmacist or psychiatry consultant can review it and place or co-sign the order through the proper override process.\n\nTell me which dose you'd like and I'll enter it."
      }
